 {"id":"CF-214","title":"Bunker provisioning is hand-run: no config-driven provisioner, no identity/auth bundle, no crier connect, no destroy — build scripts/bunker-provision.sh + skill","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Owner requirement 2026-11-04: every new project or config change today is bespoke; provisioning must be one script with CLI options plus a config file, given a target host and a config, able to destroy, carrying github + duckbrain + s3 auth to a clean working node connected to crier. Built this tick: scripts/bunker-provision.sh (--config + CLI overrides, --check/--apply/--destroy, KEY=VALUE parser that refuses shell lines, auth bundle mode 600 installed and deleted) and scripts/bunker-crier-connect.sh (identity file + inbox probe that only says CONNECTED on 200). Measured baseline on cf-node: PASS bunker_cli/server/agent/repo(af67c3b,0 dirty)/duckbrain(404=reachable); FAIL node_json, auth_github, auth_duckbrain, harness, crier 401.","acceptance_criteria":"1. scripts/bunker-provision.sh --check is read-only and every row names its measured value; scripts/bunker-provision.sh --apply is idempotent and reuses an existing agent rather than spawning a second.\n2. The config file is parsed as data: a non-KEY=VALUE line is a FAIL and is never executed; the command line overrides the file for agent, server, repo, ref, harness, namespace, crier url and token file paths.\n3. Credentials travel as files only: one bundle of mode 600 copied in, installed under $HOME/.node/auth at mode 600, and deleted on both sides; no value reaches stdout, a log or an argument.\n4. scripts/bunker-crier-connect.sh reports CONNECTED only when <crier>/agents/<agent>/inbox answers 200, and names 401/403/404/000 as distinct failures.\n5. --destroy refuses without --yes and prints the exact agent and server it would remove.\n6. tests/selftest.sh has fixtures proving the failure path: absent config, a shell line in the config, no bunker client on PATH, destroy without --yes — each exits non-zero naming its reason.\n7. skills/code-factory-bunker/SKILL.md ships with the repo and a fleet copy exists under the coding-hermes skills library; both name the same commands.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-05T00:55:50Z","updated_at":"2026-11-05T00:55:50Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"ff6db82e24fc4882367a91176c8c12d7c01e69ceb74fb580f35986c4af04c0f9"},"capability_tags":[],"commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null}
 {"id":"CF-216","title":"specs/ENVELOPE.md describes neither the shipped dispatch nor the shipped reply, and its delivery budget claim is unsupported — the numbers are 30 s lease, 30 s signature window, 35 s dispatch timeout, 3 h await","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Round-trip research 2026-11-05 (report /home/kara/code-factory-reports/crier-scheduler-roundtrip-2026-11-05.md): the envelope spec has NO delivery budget at all, and its wire shape matches neither implementation. Real numbers measured in code: crier's default inbox lease 30 s (internal/bus/handler.go:1693), per-agent signature window ±30 s, webhook budget 30 s, scheduler dispatch timeout 35 s, reply poll 10 s with a 45 s lease, and the await budget is the tick timeout (3 h live). The scheduler's reply path is internal/bus/inbox.go + internal/scheduler/dispatch_leg.go::awaitDispatchReply; crier's is POST /agents/{id}/inbox with Bearer + per-agent signature (internal/bus/dispatch.go:289,341-346, agentsig.go:167-171).","acceptance_criteria":"1. specs/ENVELOPE.md states the dispatch request shape the scheduler actually sends (route, method, auth headers, body fields) with file:line citations to internal/bus/dispatch.go.\n2. It states the reply shape a node must post, including the correlation key, and names which key survives a lost receipt row.\n3. It replaces any unsupported budget claim with the measured numbers and the file:line each comes from, or states plainly that no budget exists.\n4. A reader can construct a valid dispatch and a valid reply from the spec alone: a fixture built only from the spec passes the scheduler's correlate+ack path on a scratch bus.\n5. The spec names the two failure modes observed in production: 403 GUARD_BLOCKED on dispatch and 401 from per-agent signature enforcement, with the reason for each.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-05T02:16:40Z","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:16:40Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"c53d2d94858bec4a1932ae1d0fd6dc900db687b