 {"id":"CF-247","title":"P1 - bunker exec ignores --timeout and has a hard 300 s client stream ceiling, so any dispatch budget above 300 s cannot be honoured through one exec stream","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured twice, most recently 2026-11-04 from the control host: bunker --server bunker-las-03 exec cf-node --timeout 600 -- sh -c \"sleep 330; echo SURVIVED-330\" died with \"bunker: stream error: deadline_exceeded: Post http://100.69.3.13:10001/bunker.v1.Bunkerd/ExecAgent: context deadline exceeded (Client.Timeout exceeded while awaiting headers)\" and the shell measured real 5m0.106s. The --timeout 600 flag is ignored; the stream boundary is a hard 300 s. Seen from the scheduler side, two live dispatches failed at exactly 300.1s while the envelope claimed wall=3h0m0s, and tick_dispatch rows carry the same signature (\"exec deadline exceeded after 1800s - pass --timeout <seconds> to extend\" alongside a Client.Timeout on the same reply). crier-fleet/dispatcher.py works around it by construction - dispatch_durable() stages the task and starts it detached, and its comments call out the 300 s boundary - but the flag is still a trap: any caller that trusts --timeout to bound a run gets a silent 300 s ceiling and a deadline_exceeded error instead of its own budget.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Either a run with --timeout 900 that lasts longer than 300 s completes and returns its output, or the flag is removed from the CLI and the 300 s ceiling is documented at the call site in crier-fleet/dispatcher.py and in the bunker help text. 2. A test or probe pins the behaviour: it proves that a budget above 300 s is either honoured or refused up front, never silently truncated at 300 s. 3. The row note records the measured wall time of the settling run.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-05T13:03:13Z","updated_at":"2026-11-05T13:03:13Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"f4361881c50b23570b884c8fa21c1f5191e52746f09376fa9c5c6999a703c47f"},"capability_tags":["bunker","transport","dispatch"],"commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"worker_status":"pending","foreman_note":null}
 {"id":"CF-248","title":"P2 - cf-node checkout drift (superseded: node sits at origin/main as of 2026-11-05, verify-node.sh exits 3 UNVERIFIED pending CF-224)","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":1,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-05 by the critical-path audit: the cf-node checkout is 2 commits behind origin/main and scripts/verify-node.sh FAILs when run there. The dispatch leg hands cf-node the repo to run in, so a node two commits stale is a second source of truth: any fix landed on main is absent exactly where the work runs, and a verification script that fails on the node cannot distinguish stale checkout from broken node. Not yet attributed: whether the failure is only the stale commits or also a real node defect, which is why this row is P2 and scoped to making verify-node.sh pass against current origin/main before any conclusion is drawn about the node itself.","acceptance_criteria":"1. scripts/verify-node.sh exits 0 on cf-node against current origin/main. 2. The cf-node checkout reports git rev-list --count HEAD..origin/main as 0 after a fetch, and the row note records both the HEAD it was on and the HEAD it was brought to. 3. If verify-node.sh still fails after the checkout is current, the failure is quoted verbatim in the row note and this row is re-scoped to the real defect rather than closed.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-05T13:03:23Z","updated_at":"2026-11-05T13:04:22Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"0b5f8a286f46bdea1bb4a9a1218c83728f835a7e4df014b0837c7415683efa53"},"capability_tags":["cf-node","deployment","verification"],"commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"worker_status":"pending","foreman_note":"PREMISE DISPROVED 2026-11-05T13:02Z by live probe, minutes after this row was filed on the audit finding. Run from /home/kara/code-factory: bash scripts/verify-node.sh --agent cf-node --server bunker-las-03 --strict-freshness reports checkout_freshness PASS with branch=main head=cf50c6e19896 origin_main=cf50c6e19896 ancestor=true behind=0, so the node is NOT 2 commits behind - it is on origin/main. The script does not FAIL either: it exits 3 with VERIFY: UNVERIFIED, which is by design (nothing broken, nothing proven) because this host has no transport to the node gateway at 192.168.123.66:10800; that residual is already tracked by CF-224, so this row does not duplicate it. What closed the drift: CF-244 landed the freshness check at 8db72b3 and the sibling lane closed it at 2026-11-05T12:57:46Z (commit cf50c6e), and the node is now on that head. Why it is deferred rather than completed: no evidence run backs it and the premise is gone. Reopen only if a node reports behind > 0 or an ancestor=false in checkout_freshness.","deferred":true}
 {"id":"CF-249","title":"A real foreman directive makes the node harness run 39 minutes and return 36 bytes, and nothing records what it was doing","status":"in_progress","priority":"P1","complexity":3,"reasoning":null,"acceptance_criteria":"1. A directive that takes longer than the poll interval produces a progress record an operator can read WHILE it runs: at minimum the last tool call and its elapsed time, written at a fixed cadence.\n2. When such a run is killed, the record says what it was doing at the moment of the kill, so the failure is attributable rather than 'it hung'.\n3. The node session store shows the conversation so far on a killed run: today a 39-minute run leaves exactly one message (the user prompt) and no assistant or tool rows.\n4. A test reproduces the hang with a directive of comparable size and asserts that progress was recorded before the deadline, so this cannot regress silently.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-05T13:05:08Z","updated_at":"2026-11-05T13:50:28Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"71cf4e64109923824af218d6d68b5607f57727f6bd73c97e2539ddd989baff9f"},"capability_tags":[],"commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"worker_status":"pending","foreman_note":"Evidence, 2026-11-04. The durable path started job-1793881486-969735cc on cf-node with the real 4,046-byte foreman directive at 07:24:56 and killed it at 08:03:58: rc=124 after 2342s (39 minutes). Its out.log is 36 bytes and contains only 'session_id: 20261105_042456_716a72'. The node's state.db has exactly ONE message for that session — the user prompt — so no assistant or tool activity was persisted for the whole run. The same node answers 'ALIVE-CHECK' in seconds for a short prompt (session 20261105_050420_fe00cc), and the 400s boundary job returned BOUNDARY-CROSSED, so the harness and the job model both work; it is this class of directive that hangs. Nothing in the run can be attributed beyond 'it hung', which is why the row asks for progress records rather than more retries.\n\nImplemented and observed 2026-11-04. run.sh starts a heartbeat that appends '<UTC time> hp=<pid> <elapsed> <cmdline>' to progress.log every 20s; the dispatcher polls that line and logs it when it changes. Live example from job job-1793884459-472317b8: '13:14:48 hp=1292325 00:20 timeout 150 /home/bunker-cf-node/.local/bin/hermes chat -q Write a 600 word essay about tundra. -Q' then '00:40' on the next line, finishing rc=0 at 55s. Three defects were found and fixed while making it true: GNU timeout runs the command in its own process group (so a run.sh pgid scan reports idle for a live harness), ps --no-headers is unsupported in the node image, and ps output contains non-UTF8 bytes that crashed subprocess text=True at three call sites (now errors=replace). AC1 is met and observed; AC2 (what it was doing at the kill) follows from the same record but has not yet been captured on a killed real run; AC3 (session store shows only the user message on a killed run) is still true and unfixed.\n\nKill capture, 2026-11-04. Re-ran the real 4,046-byte directive through the durable path with the heartbeat live. Job job-1793885085-89c9fe3d: started 08:24:56, killed rc=124 at 08:49:01 after 1447s. The record names the position at the kill: '13:48:36 hp=1292809 23:41 timeout 1440 .../hermes chat -q [Scheduler tick: diag-node-1] ...' — 23m41s inside the same process, and at NO point during 24 minutes did a child process appear (no git, no python3, no bash), so no tool ran. AC2 is now met: the record says what the run was doing when it was killed. Attribution of WHY is narrowed: the node-to-proxy leg is not at fault — measured from the control host against the same proxy, 4.4KB (5.9s), 20KB (2.7s), 60KB (3.4s) and a streaming request (180 chunks, 3.4s) all return 200; the tick prefix alone returns PREFIX-OK on the node in seconds. What remains is the harness's own agent loop, which is invisible because it runs tools in-process and persists nothing until exit. Next step is harness-level logging, not more transport work."}
