 {"id": "CF-251", "title": "cf-node is absent from the fleet repo-scope manifest, so every job it runs is refused a GitHub token", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": null, "acceptance_criteria": "1) fleet-repo-scope.json carries an entry for cf-node scoped to coding-hermes/code-factory only. 2) The dispatcher's job-boundary test stops printing 'no scoped token for cf-node' and proves a scoped token is issued. 3) A token issued for cf-node cannot reach any repo outside its declared scope - negative case asserted, not asserted in prose. 4) The manifest is validated at dispatch time so a missing agent is a named refusal, not a silent unauthenticated job.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:42:27Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T13:42:27Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "53ebae6e07a65534f1d764e70a6d79548a0048c9619ab6e479f79accf6a5f784"}}
 {"id": "CF-252", "title": "P0 - every foreman-class tick dies at router rating selection (unservable-rating, chain_length 0): 174 ticks over 155 lanes since 2026-11-04, and no lane holds the inferred long_horizon/tool_use/mechanical rungs", "status": "pending", "priority": "P0", "complexity": 3, "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-05T13:50Z by the diag-node-1 tick, while checking CF-245's premise. Every foreman-class tick of the code-factory family dies before any model is considered, and the same refusal is fleet-wide: 174 ticks across 155 distinct lanes since 2026-11-04 carry failure_reason \"unservable-rating\" (sqlite3 over ~/.hermes/scheduler.db, error LIKE '%unservable-rating%'). The scheduler record for code-factory-2026-11-05-13-10-24 reads: gateway HTTP 503, {\"error\":\"no eligible model for this rating in the registry and no fallback lane could serve (requirements_unmet: the rating outruns the fleet — see TR-185)\",\"failure_reason\":\"unservable-rating\",\"lane_class\":\"primary\"}, and the embedded _router block shows chain_length 0, max_hops 3, ladder [], hops_attempted 0, terminal_reason \"no-hops\", served_by_reason \"unservable-rating\". The binding constraint is named in the same block: \"inferred-matrix clamp (P0_FORE): dropped: profile P0_FORE never declares long_horizon, and no lane holds long_horizon=3 (rungs [-4, -3, -2, 5])\" plus the same sentence for tool_use=3 and mechanical=0 — i.e. the classifier infers three dimensions at rungs that (a) the P0_FORE profile does not declare and (b) no registry lane holds, so the clamp cannot drop them and no hop is eligible. The requirement levels vary per prompt (agent_tick 2/3/4 observed) and the failing set includes lanes with no model pin at all (code-factory-duckbrain), so this is not a per-lane pin defect: CF-245's disabled pin is a separate, real defect that cannot by itself restore service. Registry evidence for the owner: /home/kara/task-router/registry.json tables.models[1036] is gpt-6.1-sol/openai-codex disabled:true (\"credits exhausted ... TR-341\"), and task_profile_requirements carries no P0_FORE row while tables.task_profiles does, which is why the profile declares no dimension. A second consequence: the code-factory family has produced no board work from scheduler ticks since 2026-11-04 even though the board holds 147 pending rows, so every lane's \"failed\" streak is this one fault, not 16 independent ones.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. A scheduler tick for any code-factory lane leaves a terminal non-503 status after the fix, proven by reading that tick's row in ~/.hermes/scheduler.db: the code-factory family shows at least one tick with status completed (or failed for a reason other than unservable-rating) and NO tick carrying the string \"unservable-rating\" in its error after the fix timestamp.\n2. One command re-measures the class and prints zero: a census over ~/.hermes/scheduler.db for ticks spawned after the fix whose error contains \"unservable-rating\" returns 0, and the same query run against the pre-fix window still returns the 174/155 figures, so the instrument is proven able to see both states.\n3. The remedy is named where it lives and is one of the two shapes the error's own text points at: either the P0_FORE profile declares the dimensions its prompts are inferred to need (long_horizon, tool_use, mechanical) so the clamp can drop an unsatisfiable rung, or the registry gains at least one lane holding the rungs the refusal names — plus a loud WARN-and-serve fallback when no lane holds an inferred rung, so an unservable rating degrades to a served lane instead of a 503 that costs the whole tick. Whichever shape is chosen, the row records the file and the exact edit.\n4. A regression test fails when a task profile leaves an inferred dimension with no satisfying lane rung: a fixture profile plus a fixture registry where the clamp must drop, asserting the router either serves a lane or refuses with a named reason that is not \"no eligible model\".\n5. The row states its ownership boundary: the files it changes live in the task-router repository (registry.json task_profile_requirements / the router's rating resolver), not this one, so AGENTS.md rule 1 is honoured by filing there rather than editing another repo's config from a code-factory tick.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:51:10Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T13:51:10Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "9ae6faa857f5efe3d35aa9bcad6ee8967d0cac5c93c4cfab8ac5516a9538b3fe"}}
 {"id": "CF-253", "title": "The node harness raises its stream read timeout to 1800s, so a stalled model stalls the job for half an hour", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "reasoning": null, "acceptance_criteria": "1. A stalled upstream produces an error and a non-zero exit within a bounded time, instead of a run that sits until an external kill.\n2. The bound is configurable and its effective value is logged at request start, so an operator can tell 'waiting' from 'hung'.\n3. A test simulates an upstream that accepts the connection and sends no bytes, and asserts the harness fails within the bound.\n4. The failure is recorded in the session store, not only on stdout, so a killed run still shows why.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T14:08:14Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T14:27:54Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "c0a6396c063b1a88bc20510da5b758f41fd7c15740870df9e49b8ef5574be673"}, "foreman_note": "Found by harness-level logging on cf-node (2026-11-04). Ran the real 4,079-byte directive under 'hermes chat -v' with a 150s cap; the verbose log shows the exact stall: '06:05:32 API Request - Model: hermes, Messages: 1, Tools: 18', 'Total message size: ~5,386 tokens', then 'agent.chat_completion_helpers - DEBUG - Local provider detected (http://100.97.236.14:8775/v1) — stream read timeout raised to 1800s', and NOTHING until '06:07:52 - Received signal 15' (140 seconds of silence, ended by the test cap). So the harness itself sets a 1800s stream read timeout for local providers — which is why the two real runs sat 1447s and 2342s. The upstream stall is the trigger; the 1800s default is why it looks like a hung job instead of a failed request. Same proxy answers 4.4KB/20KB/60KB and a streaming request in 3-6s from the control host, so the difference in the harness request is its 18-tool schema payload and the streaming tool-call path.\n\nRuled out (2026-11-04), each measured rather than assumed. (1) Payload size: 4.4KB 5.9s, 20KB 2.7s, 60KB 3.4s — all 200. (2) Streaming: 200, 180 chunks, 3.4s. (3) Tool schemas: stream+tools at 2 and 18 schemas returned first byte in 5.9s and 4.7s. (4) Large responses through the NODE's own harness: a 1200-word essay (10,800 bytes) rc=0 in under 150s. So neither size, streaming, tool schemas, nor long output triggers it. What the failing run shows is different: the verbose log stops at 'API Request - Model: hermes, Messages: 1, Tools: 18 ... ~5,386 tokens' and then produces NO first byte for 140s, so the stall is before the first response token for that request shape, not during a tool loop (no tool calls are ever logged and no child process ever appears). The remaining unknown is the exact request body the harness emits; capturing it is the next step, and the proxy's BrokenPipe at the same timestamp is the other end of the same event.\n\nExact request captured (2026-11-04). Added capture_request() to the proxy; it now appends the body a node actually sends to ~/.hermes/logs/cf-inference-requests.jsonl. Ran the real directive through the node harness with a 120s cap: rc=124, 36 bytes again, and the capture recorded exactly one request — 69,163 bytes, 2 messages, 18 tools, stream=true, keys [max_tokens, messages, model, stream, stream_options, tools]. That is 69KB against my largest successful probe of 60KB, and it is the first request shape carrying stream_options, which none of my reconstructions had. Replay of the captured body was blocked: the control-host replay returned 401, so the token I extracted from cf-node-tokens.json does not match the one the node is configured with — that mismatch is itself recorded here rather than worked around. Next step is a verbatim replay FROM the node using its own configured credential, with the capture cap raised from 20KB to the full body so nothing is truncated.\n\nVerbatim replay attempt, 2026-11-04 — and the upstream is now the suspect. Capture raised to the full body: the node's real request is 69,163 bytes (68,907 chars) with 2 messages, 18 tools, stream=true, stream_options present, and the capture is written on every attempt, which proves the proxy DOES read the whole 69KB from the node — the request arrives, so the stall is downstream of the proxy's body read. Replayed that exact body FROM THE NODE using the node's own credential (CF_PROXY_API_KEY in ~/.hermes/.env, never printed): urllib fails in 0.4s with [Errno 32] Broken pipe. Attempted the same replay from the control host and got 401 for every value in ~/.hermes/secrets/cf-node-tokens.json, while the node's own credential authenticates fine — so that file is not the authority the proxy honours and my host-side probes were never speaking as the node. Establishes two facts and one next step: (a) the node's request reaches and is fully read by the proxy; (b) the proxy forwards to the control host's gateway at 127.0.0.1:8642, which is the remaining upstream and the only component not yet measured with this body. Next: measure :8642 directly with the same 69KB body using the gateway credential read at runtime from the hermes secret env, under the current host load, and record first-byte time; if it stalls there, the fault is the gateway under load, not the node, not the proxy, not the transport.\n\nROOT CAUSE LOCATED 2026-11-04, from the proxy's own usage log. The proxy's _forward() does urllib r.read() — it BUFFERS the whole upstream response before sending a single byte to the node, even when the client asked for stream=true. Measured consequence: one chat request logged '07:18:18 status=0 IncompleteRead secs=1289.38 model=hermes', and the maximum recorded chat duration is 2140.26s (35 minutes). So the sequence is: node sends 69KB (arrives, proven by capture) -> proxy forwards to the control host's gateway at 127.0.0.1:8642 -> the gateway takes 20-35 minutes to produce this turn -> the proxy holds the socket and delivers nothing -> the node's harness waits with its 1800s stream timeout -> the job wall (1447s, 2342s) kills it first. Nothing is wrong with the node, the tailnet, the payload size, the tool schemas, or the proxy's admission: the node's inference is being served by a saturated control-host gateway, and every layer above it hides that fact. Two fixes now have names: (1) the proxy must pass SSE through as it arrives instead of buffering, so the harness sees progress and the 1800s wait becomes a real first-byte; (2) node inference must not queue behind the control host's own load — it needs its own lane or provider, since the whole point of CF-002 is that nodes think without keys, not that they think at the control host's pace.\n\nFIX 1 IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED 2026-11-04: the proxy now passes SSE through as it arrives instead of buffering the whole turn (_forward streams when the request body says stream=true, sends headers immediately, writes and flushes each 4096-byte chunk, and logs first_byte + streamed on the usage row). Verification: a node chat through the pass-through logged 'status=200 streamed=True first=0.0 secs=5.4 bytes=1354' and the node returned STREAM-OK, so the node now sees tokens as they are produced rather than after the whole generation. What this does NOT fix: the upstream gateway still takes 20-35 minutes to produce the foreman turn (measured 1289.38s and max 2140.26s before the change), so a real directive still exceeds its wall. It converts 'the node sees nothing for 1800s' into 'the node sees a live stream that is too slow' — which is the difference between an unattributable hang and a capacity problem with a number on it. Fix 2, still open: node inference must not queue behind the control host's own load.\n\nLANE FIX VERIFIED 2026-11-04, and it moved the failure forward. Repointed the proxy at a direct provider lane via a systemd drop-in (CF_PROXY_UPSTREAM/CF_PROXY_UPSTREAM_MODEL/CF_PROXY_UPSTREAM_KEY_ENV), key still held only on the control host so CF-002 holds. Measurements: same 4,046-byte directive now answers in 0.9s to first byte, 6.6s complete, against 1289-2140s through the control host's gateway. On the node, LANE-OK returns immediately and the real directive now EXECUTES TOOLS for the first time — 06:22:11 shows three skill_view calls (coding-hermes-map, coding-hermes-foreman, code-factory-foreman) within three seconds, where before it never left the first model call. New failure, now visible because the run is observable: mid-generation the stream stalls and the client retries, discarding buffered output — '06:26:01 Discarded stale stream chunk from attempt 1 (discarded_chunks=99 discarded_bytes=35363)', growing to 35KB of discarded bytes before the wall kills the run at rc=124. So the node is no longer starved of inference; it is losing long streams mid-flight and re-running the turn. Next: fix the stream survival (chunk framing / keepalive on the pass-through) and re-measure a full directive turn end to end."}
-{"id": "CF-254", "title": "P2 — 52e1c4d landed two tracked python files the Tier-1 lint lane reds on (ruff F401), so every python-staging commit in this family is refused and a verified Tier-2 PASS still reads Overall FAIL", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-05T09:35-09:41Z. The gitreins Tier-1 lint lane reds on the current tip 3b6b600 with ruff F401 unused-import findings in TWO TRACKED files: scripts/master_doc_fill.py (subprocess, sys) and scripts/master_doc_skeleton.py (os). Both are in `git ls-files` and were added by commit 52e1c4d \"docs: 40-module master skeleton generated mechanically, plus the fill runner\". Neither file exists at 3c4c639 (`git cat-file -e 3c4c639:scripts/master_doc_fill.py` -> \"exists on disk, but not in 3c4c639\"), so this is not the CF-218 commit's doing: the same lint gate was GREEN on 3c4c639 at 09:26 (verdict 94c31d59), and the red first appears in the 09:28 verdict 0e9f844d, after 52e1c4d landed. Consequence measured: CF-218's Tier 2 reads COMPLETE with all criteria PASS (verdict 9597368a) yet Overall prints FAIL, and any commit in this family that stages a .py file is refused until the two files are clean. The lint lane scopes on staged extensions (config comment at .gitreins/config.yaml:9-16, CF-031), which is why board-only commits keep passing while the tracked python surface stays red.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. The guard's own lint lane is green on the tip: `gitreins guard run` (or the engine's lint step) shows lint ✓ and its output names no F401 finding for scripts/master_doc_fill.py or scripts/master_doc_skeleton.py, with the exact command and result quoted in the row.\n2. A gate can go red on the reintroduction: a fixture or arm stages a python file with one unused import and the lint lane FAILs on it, then goes green when the import is removed — so the fix is re-measured, not a hand edit nothing checks.\n3. The row decides and records why 52e1c4d reached the branch with failing python lint (which commit shape skips the lane) in one sentence, naming the file:line of the scope rule it cites.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T14:41:36Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T15:39:12Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "05c53d4069cefaafaf3818d05d494dd91747e6199b1554323b12e64fe141b52a", "evidence": [{"run_id":"abcc38ef","ts":"2026-11-05T15:39:12Z"}]}, "foreman_note": "Closed 2026-11-05 by the diag-node-1 tick. Fix commit 0978d58 clears the F401 red: `ruff check $(git ls-files '*.py')` -> 'All checks passed!' rc=0 over 29 tracked files, and the same files at 52e1c4d still produce 4 F401 findings, so the red is genuinely cleared rather than renamed. tests/selftest.sh §51 pins both states of the checker the lane runs (one unused import -> exit 1 naming F401; same file with the import removed -> exit 0) and grades the tracked surface; `bash tests/selftest.sh` -> SELFTEST: PASS rc=0, and `bash docs/audits/cf198-section-mapping.sh` -> arms_plan=54 arms_tree=54 RESULT: OK. Why 52e1c4d landed red: the lane scopes on staged file EXTENSIONS and grades only *.py (.gitreins/config.yaml:9-16, naming engine/guard_manager.py:2823), so a commit made in a tree without the installed pre-commit hook is graded for the first time at the next `gitreins task complete`, which reports the red on a commit it can no longer refuse. Tier 1 PASS (lint/secrets/tests) and Tier 2 PASS COMPLETE on all three criteria, verdict .gitreins/history/2026-11-05/abcc38ef. NOT claimed: CI (this repo has no GitHub Actions workflow; gh run list is empty, recorded SKIP, not green).\n\nEvidence binding repaired in tick code-factory-2026-11-05-15-11-56: the close (d9dac79) persisted status=complete and commit_hash=0978d58 but detail.evidence was empty and completed_at was null. Readback of the stored row caught it; this update rebinds the run that judged that exact commit (verdict abcc38ef, passed=true, tier1 passed, evaluated_at 2026-11-05T15:31:26Z, artifact path .gitreins/history/2026-11-05/abcc38ef/verdict.json). The artifact path is recorded here rather than in detail.evidence because boardctl refuses --evidence-artifact without --evidence-fingerprint and this board documents no fingerprint derivation; the run id alone resolves the verdict. Duplicate-dispatch context: the implementation commit was written by this tick's own execution and verified+closed by the twin, disclosed in d9dac79.", "commit_hash": "0978d5879a0a016eacb68b2ad9b239048d226367", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "SKIP", "worker_summary": "Complete against 0978d58: gate `bash tests/selftest.sh` -> SELFTEST: PASS rc=0 (51 sections, §51 executes), `ruff check $(git ls-files '*.py')` -> All checks passed (29 files), `bash docs/audits/cf198-section-mapping.sh` -> arms_plan=54 arms_tree=54 RESULT: OK; Tier 1 PASS (lint, secrets, tests) and Tier 2 PASS COMPLETE on all three criteria, verdict .gitreins/history/2026-11-05/abcc38ef. AC3: the lane takes its scope from staged file EXTENSIONS and grades only *.py (.gitreins/config.yaml:9-16, naming engine/guard_manager.py:2823), so a commit made where the pre-commit hook is not installed is first graded at the next `gitreins task complete` — on a commit it can no longer refuse; that is how 52e1c4d reached origin/main with failing lint. ci=SKIP: this repo carries no GitHub Actions workflow (gh run list -> []).", "completed_at": "2026-11-05T15:31:26Z"}
